`<Admin>` is the whole editor: a rail of collections, a panel with the open collection's documents (or the open document's outline), a top bar with Save, the preview, and an inspector with the selected item's form. It's a component, so you mount it on a route of your own.

```tsx
import { Admin } from '@mortar-kit/admin';

export default function AdminRoute() {
  return (
    <Admin
      bricks={bricks}
      collections={collections}
      content={adminContent}
      previewUrl="/admin/preview"
    />
  );
}
```

`content` is a `Content`: reads plus `save` and `remove`, and `move` for reordering [orderable collections](/docs/collections#manual-order) (their document list gets drag handles and move up and down buttons). Give it uncached reads, so the admin always shows what is saved. Saves are validated again by `createContent`, and problems come back to the editor, linked to the fields they're about.

## The preview route

The preview is an iframe on your own origin, rendering `<PreviewPage>` with your bricks and styles. Each collection can have a preview component of its own: pass `layouts`, by collection name, to render its documents inside your real layouts, so a post previews with its title and date and a product as its product page. A layout gets the document (updated as the editor types) and the rendered body:

```tsx
import { PreviewPage } from '@mortar-kit/admin/preview';

const layouts = {
  posts: (doc, body) => <PostLayout post={doc}>{body}</PostLayout>,
  products: (doc) => <ProductLayout product={doc} />,
};

export default function AdminPreview() {
  return <PreviewPage bricks={bricks} collections={collections} content={freshSource} layouts={layouts} />;
}
```

A collection without a layout previews as `<DocPreview>`, when you pass `collections`: a card with the document's title, its first image, every other field under its label (dates and choices spelled out, references by their document's title, lists and objects nested), and then its body. Layouts can render `<DocPreview collection={…} doc={doc}>` themselves too, to start from it. Without `collections`, such a collection previews its body alone.

Load both routes with `lazy()`, so the admin's code is only downloaded on these routes.

## As a router route

With @solidjs/router, use `<AdminRoute>` from `@mortar-kit/admin/router` in place of `<Admin>`. It takes the same props and adds two things. The admin's location lives in your URL's search parameters (`?collection=posts&id=…`, `?view=assets` for the asset library), so documents can be linked and back and forward move between them. Router navigation that would lose unsaved changes asks first: a link, back and forward, or switching documents. A save still in flight is waited for before leaving.

```tsx
// src/routes/admin.tsx, routed at /admin
import { AdminRoute } from '@mortar-kit/admin/router';

export default function AdminPage() {
  return <AdminRoute bricks={bricks} collections={collections} content={adminContent} previewUrl="/admin/preview" />;
}
```

`confirmLeave(question)` replaces `window.confirm` for the question.

## The location in your URL, by hand

`<Admin>` keeps track of where it is itself by default. With another router, pass `location` and `onNavigate`:

```tsx
<Admin
  bricks={bricks}
  collections={collections}
  content={adminContent}
  previewUrl="/admin/preview"
  location={{ collection: params.collection ?? 'pages', id: params.id, view: params.view === 'assets' ? 'assets' : undefined }}
  onNavigate={(to) => setParams({ collection: to.collection, id: to.id ?? null, view: to.view ?? null })}
/>
```

`view: 'assets'` is the asset library.

## Assets

When `content` has `assets` (content created with a blob store, see [Assets](/docs/assets)), the rail gets an **Assets** button. It opens the library: every uploaded file, filtered by kind, with upload by button or drag and drop, copy URL, and delete, which is refused while documents use the file and lists them. Image and file fields get **Choose…**, which opens the same library as a picker filtered to what the field accepts, and uploading a single file there picks it.

## Unsaved changes

The editor guards closing the tab. `<AdminRoute>` guards navigation inside your app. With `<Admin>` and another router, listen to `onDirtyChange` and block navigation while there are unsaved changes.

## Editing one document

`<Editor>` is the editor for a single document, without the rail and the list. Give it the collection, the document, `previewUrl` and an `onSave`.
